= Justification =

== Overview ==
In epistemology, '''justification''' concerns whether a [[Belief|belief]] is appropriately supported, rational, warranted, or held on good epistemic grounds.

A justified belief can still be false. Justification is therefore not the same thing as truth.

The concept matters because [[Knowledge|knowledge]] seems to require more than accidentally having a true belief. Exactly what kind of positive epistemic standing is required remains disputed.

== Propositional and doxastic justification ==
A common distinction separates two questions.

'''Propositional justification''' asks whether a person has sufficient reason or [[Evidence|evidence]] to believe proposition p.

'''Doxastic justification''' asks whether the person's actual belief that p is based on or formed from the relevant good reasons in the appropriate way.

A person might possess excellent evidence but believe the conclusion for an unrelated bad reason. In that case, the proposition may be justified for them even though the particular belief is improperly based.

== The basing relation ==
The '''epistemic basing relation''' connects the reasons/evidence a person possesses with the belief they actually hold because of those reasons.

This is important because simply possessing good reasons somewhere in memory does not guarantee that those reasons explain or justify the belief.

Theories of basing include:
* causal accounts;
* counterfactual accounts;
* dispositional accounts;
* doxastic/meta-belief accounts.

Each tries to distinguish appropriate support from accidental causal connections.

== Internalism ==
'''Internalist''' theories hold that justification depends on factors appropriately internal or accessible to the subject.

Different internalisms require different things:
* reflective access to reasons;
* dependence only on mental states;
* availability of justification through reflection.

Internalism captures the intuition that a rational person should be able, in some sense, to answer for their belief.

== Externalism ==
'''Externalist''' theories allow epistemic justification or warrant to depend on factors outside the subject's reflective access.

For example, process reliabilism says that a belief can be justified partly because it was produced by a reliable truth-conducive process.

A person need not always know that the process is reliable for the process to contribute epistemically.

== Foundationalism ==
'''Foundationalism''' holds that some beliefs have positive epistemic standing without being inferentially justified by other beliefs.

Other justified beliefs ultimately depend on these basic beliefs.

The challenge is to explain:
* what makes foundational beliefs justified;
* how they support non-basic beliefs;
* how arbitrary foundations are avoided.

== Coherentism ==
'''Coherentism''' holds that justification depends substantially on how beliefs fit together within a coherent system.

Coherence can involve:
* logical consistency;
* explanatory integration;
* mutual support;
* inferential connections;
* avoidance of ad hoc exceptions.

A coherent system can still be globally disconnected from reality, so coherentists must explain how experience and world-directed constraints enter the system.

== Evidentialism ==
'''Evidentialism''' holds that a subject's justified attitudes are determined by their evidence.

Different evidentialists disagree about:
* what evidence is;
* whether evidence must be accessible;
* whether practical stakes affect epistemic justification;
* how evidence determines permissible confidence.

== Reliabilism ==
'''Reliabilism''' evaluates belief partly through the truth-conduciveness of the process that produced or sustains it.

Perception, memory, or a measurement procedure may provide justification if they function reliably in the relevant environment.

Reliabilist theories face questions about which process type and reference class should be evaluated.

== Defeaters ==
A belief that is initially justified may lose that status when a '''defeater''' appears.

A rebutting defeater supports the opposite conclusion.

An undercutting defeater attacks the connection between the evidence and the belief—for example, learning that an instrument was malfunctioning.

Defeaters make justification dynamic rather than permanent.

== Justification and knowledge ==
The traditional justified-true-belief analysis treated justification as a component of knowledge.

Gettier cases show that ordinary justification plus truth and belief can coexist with epistemic luck.

Modern epistemology therefore studies stronger ideas such as warrant, safety, reliability, competence, and anti-luck conditions.

== Epistemic notes ==
"Justification" here means epistemic justification. Legal, moral, political, or practical justification are different concepts that may use related language.

